.Dd May 25, 2026
.Dt XTC_STRERROR 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_strerror ,
.Nm xtc_err_errno ,
.Nm xtc_err_from_errno ,
.Nm XTC_E_SYS
.Nd describe an xtc error code; the system-error band
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.Ft const char *
.Fn xtc_strerror "int xtc_err"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_err_errno "int rc"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_err_from_errno "int neg_or_pos_errno"
.Fd #define XTC_E_SYS_BASE (-4096)
.Fd #define XTC_E_SYS(e) (XTC_E_SYS_BASE - (e))
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn xtc_strerror
function returns a stable English description of the xtc error code
.Fa xtc_err .
.Pp
Recognised values are
.Dv XTC_OK
and the
.Dv XTC_E_*
codes declared in
.In xtc.h .
For any code outside the recognised set the function returns the
string
.Dq unknown ;
it never returns
.Dv NULL .
.Pp
The returned pointer is to static storage and must not be freed.
The exact text for each known code is part of the API contract for
log-message stability and changes only on a major version bump.
.Pp
The codes, their values, and the text each one reads as:
.Bl -column "XTC_E_RESOURCE" "-14" -offset indent
.It Sy Code Ta Sy Value Ta Sy Text
.It Dv XTC_OK Ta 0 Ta Dq ok
.It Dv XTC_E_INVAL Ta \-1 Ta Dq invalid argument
.It Dv XTC_E_NOMEM Ta \-2 Ta Dq out of memory
.It Dv XTC_E_NOSYS Ta \-3 Ta Dq not implemented on this platform
.It Dv XTC_E_RANGE Ta \-4 Ta Dq numeric out of range
.It Dv XTC_E_AGAIN Ta \-5 Ta Dq resource temporarily unavailable
.It Dv XTC_E_INTERNAL Ta \-6 Ta Dq internal invariant violation
.It Dv XTC_E_RESOURCE Ta \-7 Ta Dq resource cap reached
.It Dv XTC_E_DEADLK Ta \-8 Ta Dq deadlock victim
.It Dv XTC_E_VERSION Ta \-9 Ta Dq version mismatch
.It Dv XTC_E_ABORTED Ta \-10 Ta Dq operation aborted
.It Dv XTC_E_NOTFOUND Ta \-11 Ta Dq requested item does not exist
.It Dv XTC_E_IO Ta \-12 Ta Dq I/O error
.It Dv XTC_E_EXIST Ta \-13 Ta Dq already exists
.It Dv XTC_E_STATE Ta \-14 Ta Dq not allowed in this state or context
.El
.Pp
.Dv XTC_E_EXIST
and
.Dv XTC_E_STATE
appeared in xtc 2.2.
.Ss The system-error band
A call that fails with an operating system error that no
.Dv XTC_E_*
code names returns
.Fn XTC_E_SYS errno ,
the value
.Dv XTC_E_SYS_BASE
minus the
.Va errno .
The band lies below every
.Dv XTC_E_*
code, so a system error can never be read as one.
A raw negative
.Va errno
would collide:
.Li -EIO
equals
.Dv XTC_E_AGAIN
and
.Li -EBADF
equals
.Dv XTC_E_VERSION .
.Xr xtc_aio 3
and
.Xr xtc_iosched 3
return the band since 2.2.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_strerror
renders a code in the band as
.Dq system error:
followed by the C library's text for the
.Va errno ,
obtained with the thread-safe
.Fn strerror_r
.Pq Fn strerror_s No on Windows .
That string lives in a per-thread buffer and stays valid until the
same thread calls
.Fn xtc_strerror
again; every other result is static.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_err_errno
returns the
.Va errno
inside the band, and 0 for any value outside it
.Pq Dv XTC_OK , an Dv XTC_E_* No code, a byte count .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_err_from_errno
returns
.Fn XTC_E_SYS e
for an
.Va errno
given with either sign, and
.Dv XTC_OK
for 0, so code that wraps its own system calls can report in the
same space.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_strerror
returns a non-NULL pointer to a NUL-terminated string.
.Fn xtc_err_errno
returns a positive
.Va errno
or 0.
.Fn xtc_err_from_errno
returns a value in the band,
.Dv XTC_OK ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_INTERNAL
for a value larger than any
.Va errno
.Pq above 65535 .
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#include <stdio.h>
#include <xtc.h>

void log_rc(int rc) {
        if (rc != XTC_OK)
                fprintf(stderr, "xtc error: %s\en", xtc_strerror(rc));
}

/* Retry a write the disk refused for lack of space, after a cleanup. */
int n = xtc_aio_pwrite(fd, buf, len, off);
if (n < 0 && xtc_err_errno(n) == ENOSPC && free_some_space() == 0)
        n = xtc_aio_pwrite(fd, buf, len, off);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_aio 3 ,
.Xr xtc_version_string 3 ,
.Xr xtc 7
.Sh HISTORY
.Fn xtc_strerror
first appeared in xtc 0.0.1;
.Fn xtc_err_errno ,
.Fn xtc_err_from_errno
and the band in xtc 2.2.
